// 2025 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2025\2025 explanation.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2025, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"With reference to investments, consider the following:\nI. BondsII. Hedge FundsIII. StocksIV. Venture Capital\nHow many of the above are treated as Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Hedge Funds and Venture Capital Funds can fall under the framework of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) regulated by SEBI.\nBonds and stocks are conventional investment instruments and are not, by themselves, AIFs.\nTherefore, two of the listed investment types are treated as AIFs.","revisionPoints":"AIF → Privately pooled investment vehicle\nHedge Funds + Venture Capital → AIF examples\nStocks + Bonds → Conventional investment instruments\nRevise:\nCategory I, II and III AIFs\nPrivate Equity\nMutual Funds\nREITs and InvITs"},
{"no":2,"text":"Which of the following are the sources of income for the Reserve Bank of India?\nI. Buying and selling Government bondsII. Buying and selling foreign currencyIII. Pension fund managementIV. Lending to private companiesV. Printing and distributing currency notes\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"I and II only","B":"II, III and IV","C":"I, III, IV and V","D":"I, II and V","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The RBI earns income from its financial operations, including buying and selling government securities and foreign currencies.\nPension fund management and lending directly to private companies are not normal sources of RBI income.\nAlthough the RBI manages currency issuance, the cost of printing currency is an expenditure, not a source of income.\nTherefore, Statements I and II are correct.","revisionPoints":"RBI Income → Securities + Forex operations + Interest income\nRevise:\nRBI balance sheet\nMonetary policy operations\nOpen Market Operations (OMO)\nForeign exchange operations\nSeigniorage"},
{"no":3,"text":"With reference to the Government of India, consider the following information:\nOrganization\nFunction\nWorks under\nI. Directorate of Enforcement\nEnforcement of the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018\nInternal Security Division-I, Ministry of Home Affairs\nII. Directorate of Revenue Intelligence\nEnforces the provisions of the Customs Act, 1962\nDepartment of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\nIII. Directorate General of Systems and Data Management\nCarries out big data analytics to assist tax officers and policy\nDepartment of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\nIn how many of the above rows is the information correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Row II is correctly matched.\nThe Directorate of Revenue Intelligence (DRI) functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance, and deals with enforcement of customs and anti-smuggling laws.\nThe Directorate of Enforcement (ED) does not work under the Ministry of Home Affairs. It functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance.\nThe Directorate General of Systems and Data Management is also associated with the indirect tax administration framework, but the given matching is not correct in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"ED → Department of Revenue → Ministry of Finance\nDRI → Department of Revenue → Ministry of Finance\nRevise:\nED\nDRI\nDGGI\nCBI\nNIA\nFIU-IND\nParent ministries and functions"},
{"no":4,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The Reserve Bank of India mandates all the listed companies in India to submit a Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR).\nStatement II: In India, a company submitting a BRSR makes disclosures in the report that are largely non-financial in nature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"BRSR is associated with SEBI, not the RBI.\nIt is a sustainability-related disclosure framework for listed entities.\nBRSR disclosures largely cover Environmental, Social and Governance (ESG) aspects.\nThese disclosures are therefore predominantly non-financial in nature.\nHence, only Statement II is correct.","revisionPoints":"BRSR → SEBI → ESG disclosures\nRevise:\nESG\nBRSR Core\nSustainability reporting\nSEBI's role in corporate disclosures"},
{"no":5,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In India, income from allied agricultural activities like poultry farming and wool rearing in rural areas is exempted from any tax.\nStatement II: In India, rural agricultural land is not considered a capital asset under the provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.","C":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.","D":"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Income from activities such as poultry farming and wool rearing is not automatically treated as agricultural income merely because the activities are carried out in rural areas.\nTherefore, Statement I is incorrect.\nRural agricultural land, subject to the conditions specified under the Income-tax Act, is generally excluded from the definition of a capital asset.\nHence, Statement II is correct.","revisionPoints":"Agricultural income ≠ Every rural income\nRevise:\nAgricultural income\nAllied agricultural activities\nRural agricultural land\nCapital assets\nTaxation of agricultural income"},
{"no":6,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. India has joined the Minerals Security Partnership as a member.\nII. India is a resource-rich country in all the 30 critical minerals that it has identified.\nIII. The Parliament in 2023 amended the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 empowering the Central Government to exclusively auction mining leases and composite licences for certain critical minerals.","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"India joined the Minerals Security Partnership (MSP).\nHowever, India is not resource-rich in all the critical minerals it has identified and remains dependent on imports for several of them.\nThe 2023 amendment to the MMDR Act gave the Central Government powers relating to auction of mining leases and composite licences for specified critical minerals.\nTherefore, Statements I and III are correct.","revisionPoints":"Critical Minerals → Economic + Strategic Security\nRevise:\nIndia's critical mineral list\nMinerals Security Partnership\nMMDR Act, 2023\nMineral import dependence"},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: As regards returns from an investment in a company, generally, bondholders are considered to be relatively at lower risk than stockholders.\nStatement II: Bondholders are lenders to a company whereas stockholders are its owners.\nStatement III: For repayment purpose, bondholders are prioritized over stockholders by a company.","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement I explains Statement II.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Bondholders are creditors/lenders of the company, whereas stockholders are owners.\nIn case of liquidation, creditors generally have priority over equity shareholders for repayment.\nTherefore, bonds generally carry lower risk than stocks, although the exact risk depends on the type of security.","revisionPoints":"Bondholder → Lender/Creditor → Higher repayment priority\nStockholder → Owner → Residual claim\nRevise:\nDebt vs Equity\nRisk-return relationship\nSecured and unsecured bonds\nLiquidation priority"},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. India accounts for a very large portion of all equity option contracts traded globally.\nII. India's stock market has grown rapidly in the recent past even overtaking Hong Kong's at some point of time.\nIII. There is no regulatory body either to warn small investors about the risks of options trading or to act on unregistered financial advisors in this regard.","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"India has become one of the world's largest markets for equity derivatives/options trading.\nIndia's stock market has also grown significantly and has, at times, surpassed Hong Kong in market capitalisation.\nStatement III is incorrect because SEBI regulates India's securities market and has measures relating to investor protection and unregistered financial advisers.\nTherefore, Statements I and II are correct.","revisionPoints":"SEBI → Securities Market Regulator\nRevise:\nFutures vs Options\nDerivatives\nOptions trading risks\nSEBI\nInvestor protection mechanisms"},
{"no":9,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Circular economy reduces the emissions of greenhouse gases.\nII. Circular economy reduces the use of raw materials as inputs.\nIII. Circular economy reduces wastage in the production process.","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Sustainable Development","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"A circular economy aims to keep resources and products in use for as long as possible through:\nReuse\nRepair\nRefurbishment\nRecycling\nIt reduces the demand for new raw materials and reduces waste.\nBoth can contribute to lower greenhouse-gas emissions, but the direct relationship between reduced raw-material extraction and emissions is stronger.\nReduced production wastage does not necessarily explain emissions reduction in every case.\nHence, Statement II and III are correct, but only Statement II directly explains Statement I.","revisionPoints":"Linear Economy → Take → Make → Use → Dispose\nCircular Economy → Reduce → Reuse → Repair → Recycle\nRevise:\nResource efficiency\nWaste management\n3R/5R\nSustainable production\nClimate change"},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Capital receipts create a liability or cause a reduction in the assets of the Government.\nII. Borrowings and disinvestment are capital receipts.\nIII. Interest received on loans creates a liability of the Government.","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Capital receipts either create a liability for the government or reduce its assets.\nBorrowings create a liability.\nDisinvestment reduces the government's ownership/assets.\nInterest received on loans is a revenue receipt and does not create a liability for the government.\nTherefore, Statements I and II are correct.","revisionPoints":"Capital Receipt → Creates Liability OR Reduces Asset\nBorrowing → Liability ↑\nDisinvestment → Asset ↓\nInterest received → Revenue Receipt\nRevise:\nCapital vs Revenue Receipts\nFiscal Deficit\nGovernment Borrowing\nDisinvestment"},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following statements about Raja Ram Mohan Roy:\nI. He possessed great love and respect for the traditional philosophical systems of the East.\nII. He desired his countrymen to accept the rational and scientific approach and the principle of human dignity and social equality of all men and women.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy combined respect for India's traditional philosophical and religious traditions with an emphasis on reason, rationalism and modern scientific thought. He strongly advocated human dignity, social equality and social reform, including the abolition of Sati.","revisionPoints":"Raja Ram Mohan Roy → Tradition + Reason + Social Reform\nBrahmo Samaj\nAbolition of Sati\nWomen's rights\nModern education\nBengal Renaissance"},
{"no":12,"text":"Consider the following subjects with regard to the Non-Cooperation Programme:\nI. Boycott of law courts and foreign cloth\nII. Observance of strict non-violence\nIII. Retention of titles and honours without using them in public\nIV. Establishment of Panchayats for settling disputes\nHow many of the above were parts of the Non-Cooperation Programme?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Non-Cooperation Programme included:\nBoycott of law courts and foreign cloth\nStrict adherence to non-violence\nEstablishment of panchayats for settling disputes\nHowever, the programme called for the surrender of titles and honours, not their retention.","revisionPoints":"Boycott → Schools + Colleges + Courts + Foreign Cloth\nSurrender → Titles and honours\nConstructive work → Panchayats + Khadi + National institutions\nFoundation → Strict non-violence\nRevise Gandhi's Non-Cooperation Movement, Khilafat Movement, Chauri Chaura and withdrawal of the movement."},
{"no":13,"text":"The irrigation device called 'Araghatta' was","A":"A water bag made of leather pulled over a pulley","B":"A large wheel with earthen pots tied to the outer ends of its spokes","C":"A large earthen pot driven by bullocks","D":"A large water bucket pulled up by rope directly by hand","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Araghatta was a traditional water-lifting device consisting of a large wheel fitted with pots. As the wheel rotated, the pots lifted water from a lower level and discharged it at a higher level for irrigation.","revisionPoints":"Araghatta → Wheel + Earthen Pots → Water Lifting\nTraditional irrigation systems\nAncient water-management techniques\nWells and tanks\nAgricultural technology in early India"},
{"no":14,"text":"Who among the following rulers in ancient India had assumed the titles 'Mattavilasa', 'Vichitrachitta' and 'Gunabhara'?","A":"Mahendravarman I","B":"Simhavishnu","C":"Narasimhavarman I","D":"Simhavarman","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Mahendravarman I, the Pallava ruler, was a patron of art, architecture and literature. He used titles such as Mattavilasa, Vichitrachitta and Gunabhara. He is also associated with the early development of Pallava rock-cut architecture.","revisionPoints":"Mahendravarman I → Pallava → Rock-cut architecture + Literature + Art\nNarasimhavarman I\nMamallapuram\nPancha Rathas\nShore Temple\nPallava architecture"},
{"no":15,"text":"Fa-hien (Faxian), the Chinese pilgrim, travelled to India during the reign of","A":"Samudragupta","B":"Chandragupta II","C":"Kumaragupta I","D":"Skandagupta","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Chinese Buddhist pilgrim Fa-hien (Faxian) travelled to India during the reign of Chandragupta II of the Gupta dynasty, around the early fifth century CE. He came primarily to study and collect Buddhist texts and recorded observations about Indian society.","revisionPoints":"Fa-hien → Chandragupta II → Gupta Period\nFa-hien's travel account\nXuanzang → Harshavardhana\nYijing → Buddhist centres in India\nChinese travellers and their accounts"},
{"no":16,"text":"Who among the following led a successful military campaign against the kingdom of Srivijaya, the powerful maritime State, which ruled the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and the neighbouring islands?","A":"Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta)","B":"Prataparudra (Kakatiya)","C":"Rajendra I (Chola)","D":"Vishnuvardhana (Hoysala)","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Rajendra I, the Chola ruler, launched a major naval expedition against Srivijaya in Southeast Asia. The campaign demonstrated the Cholas' strong naval capabilities and their maritime commercial interests.","revisionPoints":"Rajendra I → Chola → Naval Expedition → Srivijaya\nChola naval power\nOverseas trade\nSoutheast Asian connections\nChola administration\nChola temple architecture"},
{"no":17,"text":"With reference to ancient India (600–322 BC), consider the following pairs:\nTerritorial Region\nRiver flowing in the region\nI. Asmaka\nGodavari\nII. Kamboja\nVipas\nIII. Avanti\nMahanadi\nIV. Kosala\nSarayu\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The correctly matched pairs are:\nAsmaka — Godavari ✅\nKosala — Sarayu ✅\nThe Kamboja — Vipas pairing is not correct in this context, while Avanti was associated primarily with the Narmada, not the Mahanadi.","revisionPoints":"Asmaka → Godavari\nKosala → Sarayu\nAvanti → Narmada\nRevise the 16 Mahajanapadas\nImportant capitals, geographical locations and associated rivers"},
{"no":18,"text":"The first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya, a music training school, was set up in 1901 by Vishnu Digambar Paluskar in","A":"Delhi","B":"Gwalior","C":"Ujjain","D":"Lahore","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Vishnu Digambar Paluskar, an important figure in the revival and institutionalisation of Indian classical music, established the first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya in Lahore in 1901.","revisionPoints":"Vishnu Digambar Paluskar → Gandharva Mahavidyalaya → Lahore → 1901\nHindustani classical music\nCarnatic music\nImportant musicians\nInstitutions associated with Indian cultural history"},
{"no":19,"text":"Ashokan inscriptions suggest that the 'Pradeshika', 'Rajuka' and 'Yukta' were important officers at the","A":"Village-level administration","B":"District-level administration","C":"Provincial administration","D":"Level of the central administration","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Pradeshika, Rajuka and Yukta were important officials associated with district-level administration during the Mauryan period. Ashoka's inscriptions provide valuable information about their administrative responsibilities.","revisionPoints":"Pradeshika + Rajuka + Yukta → District Administration\nMauryan administrative structure\nAshokan Dhamma\nAshokan inscriptions\nMauryan officers and the nature of the Mauryan state"},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following statements in respect of the Non-Cooperation Movement:\nI. The Congress declared the attainment of 'Swaraj' by all legitimate and peaceful means to be its objective.\nII. It was to be implemented in stages with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes for the next stage only if 'Swaraj' did not come within a year and the Government resorted to repression.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Congress adopted the attainment of Swaraj through legitimate and peaceful means as the objective of the Non-Cooperation programme.\nThe movement was planned in stages. Civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes were envisaged as a later stage if Swaraj was not achieved within the expected period and the Government continued with repression.","revisionPoints":"Non-Cooperation → Boycott → Constructive Work → Civil Disobedience as the next stage\nNagpur Session, 1920\nSwaraj\nKhilafat–Non-Cooperation alliance\nChauri Chaura\nWithdrawal of the Non-Cooperation Movement\nRemember:\nRashtriya Gokul Mission → Indigenous cattle → Conservation + Genetic improvement\nAlso revise animal husbandry schemes, livestock breeds, dairy development and indigenous cattle conservation."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nI. AustriaII. BulgariaIII. CroatiaIV. SerbiaV. SwedenVI. North Macedonia\nHow many of the above are members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)?","A":"Only three","B":"Only four","C":"Only five","D":"All the six","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Among the six countries, Bulgaria, Croatia, Sweden and North Macedonia are NATO members. Austria and Serbia are not NATO members.","revisionPoints":"NATO members → Bulgaria + Croatia + Sweden + North Macedonia\nAustria → NATO non-member\nSerbia → NATO non-member\nRevise NATO expansion and European non-NATO countries."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nI. BoliviaII. BrazilIII. ColombiaIV. EcuadorV. ParaguayVI. Venezuela\nAndes Mountains pass through how many of the above countries?","A":"Only two","B":"Only three","C":"Only four","D":"Only five","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Andes Mountains pass through Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador and Venezuela among the countries listed. They do not pass through Brazil or Paraguay.","revisionPoints":"Andes → Venezuela → Colombia → Ecuador → Peru → Bolivia → Chile → Argentina\nRevise major mountain ranges of South America.\nLearn the countries through which major geographical features pass."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following water bodies:\nI. Lake TanganyikaII. Lake Tonlé SapIII. Patos Lagoon\nThrough how many of them does the Equator pass?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Equator does not pass through any of these three water bodies.\nLake Tanganyika lies south of the Equator.\nTonlé Sap is located in Cambodia.\nPatos Lagoon is in southern Brazil.","revisionPoints":"Equator → 0° latitude\nRevise countries crossed by the Equator.\nRevise the Tropic of Cancer and Tropic of Capricorn.\nLocate major lakes and water bodies on the world map."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements about turmeric during the year 2022–23:\nI. India is the largest producer and exporter of turmeric in the world.\nII. More than 30 varieties of turmeric are grown in India.\nIII. Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are major turmeric-producing States in India.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"India is a major global producer and exporter of turmeric, and Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are among the important turmeric-producing States.\nStatement II is considered incorrect in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"Turmeric → India → Major producer + exporter\nMajor turmeric-producing States\nMajor spice-producing regions\nGI-tagged agricultural products\nIndia's spice exports"},
{"no":25,"text":"Which of the following are the evidences of the phenomenon of continental drift?\nI. The belt of ancient rocks from the Brazil coast matches with those from Western Africa.\nII. The gold deposits of Ghana are derived from the Brazil plateau when the two continents lay side by side.\nIII. The Gondwana system of sediments from India is known to have its counterparts in six different landmasses of the Southern Hemisphere.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"I and III only","B":"I and II only","C":"I, II and III","D":"II and III only","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The statements describe important geological evidence supporting continental drift:\nMatching ancient rock formations across the Atlantic.\nSimilar mineral deposits on continents that were once joined.\nThe presence of comparable Gondwana sedimentary formations across several Southern Hemisphere landmasses.","revisionPoints":"Evidence for Continental Drift:\nJigsaw fit of continents\nMatching rock formations\nSimilar mineral deposits\nFossil evidence\nPaleoclimatic evidence\nGondwana deposits\nAlso revise Alfred Wegener, Pangaea, Laurasia, Gondwana and Plate Tectonics."},
{"no":26,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The amount of dust particles in the atmosphere is more in subtropical and temperate areas than in equatorial and polar regions.\nStatement II: Subtropical and temperate areas have less dry winds.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.","C":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.","D":"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Dust particles are relatively abundant in subtropical and temperate regions, partly because of dry conditions, deserts and strong winds.\nStatement II is incorrect because these regions generally experience more dry winds, rather than less dry winds.","revisionPoints":"Dry regions + Strong winds → More atmospheric dust\nAtmospheric aerosols\nDust and humidity\nWinds and atmospheric circulation\nEffects of aerosols on climate"},
{"no":27,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In January, in the Northern Hemisphere, the isotherms bend equatorward while crossing the landmasses, and poleward while crossing the oceans.\nStatement II: In January, the air over the oceans is warmer than that over the landmasses in the Northern Hemisphere.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.","C":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.","D":"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"January is winter in the Northern Hemisphere. Land cools more rapidly than water, while oceans retain heat. Therefore, land is relatively colder and oceans are relatively warmer.\nAs a result, isotherms bend equatorward over colder landmasses and poleward over warmer oceans.","revisionPoints":"January → NH winter → Land colder → Ocean warmer\nIsotherms\nContinentality\nMaritime influence\nSeasonal temperature distribution"},
{"no":28,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In the context of effect of water on rocks, chalk is known as a very permeable rock whereas clay is known as quite an impermeable or least permeable rock.\nStatement II: Chalk is porous and hence can absorb water.\nStatement III: Clay is not at all porous.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Statement II is correct: Chalk is porous and can absorb water, and its interconnected pores allow water to pass through relatively easily.\nStatement III is incorrect: Clay is porous, but its extremely fine particles produce very small pore spaces. Therefore, water moves through clay very slowly, making it poorly permeable.","revisionPoints":"Porosity ≠ Permeability\nChalk → Porous + Relatively permeable\nClay → Porous + Very low permeability\nDistinguish between absorption, porosity and permeability."},
{"no":29,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Without the atmosphere, temperature would be well below the freezing point everywhere on the Earth's surface.\nII. Heat absorbed and trapped by the atmosphere maintains our planet's average temperature.\nIII. Atmospheric gases, like carbon dioxide, are particularly good at absorbing and trapping radiation.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and III only","B":"I and II only","C":"I, II and III","D":"II and III only","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The atmosphere plays an important role in regulating Earth's temperature through the greenhouse effect.\nGreenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide absorb outgoing infrared radiation and re-emit part of it, helping maintain Earth's average surface temperature. Without the atmosphere, Earth's average temperature would be much lower.","revisionPoints":"Atmosphere → Greenhouse effect → Heat retention → Habitable Earth\nGreenhouse gases\nEarth's energy balance\nAlbedo\nGlobal warming\nAtmospheric absorption and radiation"},
{"no":30,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Rashtriya Gokul Mission:\nI. It is important for the upliftment of the rural poor as majority of low producing indigenous animals are with small and marginal farmers and landless labourers.\nII. It was initiated to promote indigenous cattle and buffalo rearing and conservation in a scientific and holistic manner.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Rashtriya Gokul Mission focuses on the development, conservation and genetic improvement of indigenous cattle breeds and promoting their scientific management.\nStatement II correctly describes the objective of the mission, while Statement I is incorrect as framed.","revisionPoints":"Rashtriya Gokul Mission → Indigenous cattle development + conservation\nGenetic improvement of indigenous breeds\nScientific cattle breeding and management\nImportance of livestock in rural livelihoods\nRevise major livestock-development schemes in India."},
{"no":31,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Studies indicate that carbon dioxide emissions from the cement industry account for more than 5% of global carbon emissions.\nStatement II: Silica-bearing clay is mixed with limestone while manufacturing cement.\nStatement III: Limestone is converted into lime during clinker production for cement manufacturing.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Cement production uses limestone and silica-bearing materials such as clay. During clinker production, limestone undergoes calcination, releasing carbon dioxide while forming lime. This process contributes significantly to the industry's emissions.","revisionPoints":"Cement → Limestone + Clay → Clinker → CO₂ emissions\nIndustrial emissions\nDecarbonisation of cement\nCarbon capture\nLow-carbon cement technologies"},
{"no":32,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: At COP28, India refrained from signing the Declaration on Climate and Health.\nStatement II: The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health is a binding declaration; and if signed, it becomes mandatory to decarbonize the health sector.\nStatement III: If India's health sector is decarbonized, the resilience of its healthcare system may be compromised.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health was not legally binding. Signing it did not create a mandatory legal obligation to decarbonize the health sector. Decarbonisation of healthcare can also contribute to resilience rather than necessarily compromising it. Hence, Statements II and III are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Climate + Health → Heat + Diseases + Healthcare resilience + Emissions\nCOP28\nClimate-health nexus\nClimate-resilient healthcare\nInternational climate declarations"},
{"no":33,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Scientific studies suggest that a shift is taking place in the Earth's rotation axis.\nStatement II: Solar flares and associated coronal mass ejections bombarded the Earth's outermost atmosphere with tremendous amount of energy.\nStatement III: As the Earth's polar ice melts, the water tends to move towards the equator.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Changes in the distribution of Earth's mass can influence its rotation and the position of its rotational axis. Melting polar ice redistributes water toward lower latitudes and can affect Earth's rotation. Solar activity does not provide the explanation for this particular shift.","revisionPoints":"Earth's rotation → Mass distribution matters\nPolar ice melt\nRedistribution of water\nEarth's rotational axis\nSea-level rise\nEarth's rotation and mass distribution"},
{"no":34,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change is frequently discussed in global discussions on sustainable development and climate change.\nStatement II: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change sets out the principles of carbon markets.\nStatement III: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change intends to promote inter-country non-market strategies to reach their climate targets.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Article 6 provides a framework for international cooperation in achieving climate goals, including market and non-market approaches. Statement II is broadly correct because Article 6 covers mechanisms related to international carbon markets. Statement III does not accurately describe the specific scope of Article 6.","revisionPoints":"Paris Agreement Article 6 → International cooperation\nArticle 6.2 → Cooperative approaches\nArticle 6.4 → Mechanism for emission reductions\nArticle 6.8 → Non-market approaches\nCarbon markets\nCarbon credits"},
{"no":35,"text":"Which one of the following launched the 'Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific'?","A":"The Asian Development Bank (ADB)","B":"The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)","C":"The New Development Bank (NDB)","D":"The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD)","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Asian Development Bank (ADB) launched the Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific to help mobilize finance for nature-positive investments and address biodiversity and climate-related challenges.","revisionPoints":"Nature Solutions Finance Hub → ADB → Asia &amp; Pacific\nMultilateral development banks\nADB\nAIIB\nNDB\nEnvironmental financing mechanisms"},
{"no":36,"text":"With reference to 'Direct Air Capture', an emerging technology, which of the following statements is/are correct?\nI. It can be used as a way of carbon sequestration.\nII. It can be a valuable approach for plastic production and in food processing.\nIII. In aviation, it can be a source of carbon for combining with hydrogen to create synthetic low-carbon fuel.","A":"I and II only","B":"III only","C":"I, II and III","D":"None of the above statements is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Direct Air Capture (DAC) removes carbon dioxide directly from atmospheric air. Captured CO₂ can be stored for carbon sequestration or used as a feedstock in industries such as food processing and plastics. It can also be combined with hydrogen to produce synthetic fuels, including fuels relevant to aviation.","revisionPoints":"DAC → Capture CO₂ from air → Store OR Reuse\nCarbon Capture and Storage (CCS)\nCarbon Capture and Utilisation (CCU)\nGreen hydrogen\nSynthetic fuels\nCarbon sequestration"},
{"no":37,"text":"Regarding Peacock tarantula (Gooty tarantula), consider the following statements:\nI. It is an omnivorous crustacean.\nII. Its natural habitat in India is only limited to some forest areas.\nIII. In its natural habitat, it is an arboreal species.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"I and III","C":"II only","D":"II and III","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Peacock tarantula, also known as the Gooty tarantula, is a spider, not a crustacean. It has a restricted natural distribution in certain forest habitats of India and is arboreal, meaning it lives primarily in trees.","revisionPoints":"Gooty Tarantula → Spider → Arboreal → Restricted Indian habitat\nEndemic species\nIUCN status\nThreatened species\nBiodiversity hotspots of India"},
{"no":38,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Carbon dioxide (CO₂) emissions in India are less than 0.5 t CO₂/capita.\nII. In terms of CO₂ emissions from fuel combustion, India ranks second in the Asia-Pacific region.\nIII. Electricity and heat producers are the largest sources of CO₂ emissions in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I and III only","B":"II only","C":"II and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"India's per-capita CO₂ emissions are well above 0.5 tonnes, making Statement I incorrect. India is a major emitter in the Asia-Pacific region, and electricity and heat production constitute a major source of CO₂ emissions.","revisionPoints":"India → High total emissions but comparatively lower per-capita emissions than many developed countries\nTotal emissions vs per-capita emissions\nEnergy-sector emissions\nElectricity and heat production\nIndia's climate commitments"},
{"no":39,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nPlant\nDescription\nI. Cassava\nWoody shrub\nII. Ginger\nHerb with pseudostem\nIII. Malabar spinach\nHerbaceous climber\nIV. Mint\nAnnual shrub\nV. Papaya\nWoody shrub\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only two","B":"Only three","C":"Only four","D":"All the five","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The correctly described plants are:\nCassava → Woody shrub ✅\nGinger → Herb with pseudostem ✅\nMalabar spinach → Herbaceous climber ✅\nMint is generally a perennial herb, not an annual shrub. Papaya is a large herbaceous plant, not a woody shrub.","revisionPoints":"Cassava → Woody shrub\nGinger → Herbaceous plant with pseudostem\nMalabar spinach → Climber\nMint → Perennial herb\nPapaya → Large herbaceous plant\nPlant morphology\nEconomic botany\nBotanical characteristics of common crops"},
{"no":40,"text":"With reference to the planet Earth, consider the following statements:\nI. Rain forests produce more oxygen than that produced by oceans.\nII. Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50% of the world's oxygen.\nIII. Well-oxygenated surface water contains several folds higher oxygen than that in atmospheric air.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"II only","C":"I and III","D":"None of the above statements is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria are responsible for roughly half of global oxygen production. Therefore, Statement II is correct.\nRainforests do not produce more oxygen than oceans, and oxygen concentration in water is much lower than in the atmosphere. Hence, Statements I and III are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Oceans → Phytoplankton → Major share of global oxygen production\nPhytoplankton\nMarine ecosystems\nOcean productivity\nCarbon cycle\nOxygen cycle"},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following types of vehicles:\nI. Full battery electric vehiclesII. Hydrogen fuel cell vehiclesIII. Fuel cell-electric hybrid vehicles\nHow many of the above are considered as alternative powertrain vehicles?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Automobile Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Alternative powertrain vehicles use propulsion systems other than conventional internal-combustion engines. Battery electric, hydrogen fuel-cell and fuel-cell electric hybrid vehicles are all alternative powertrain vehicles.","revisionPoints":"Alternative Powertrain → Battery Electric + Hydrogen Fuel Cell + Fuel Cell Hybrid\nBEV\nHEV\nPHEV\nHydrogen fuel-cell vehicles\nConventional internal-combustion engines"},
{"no":42,"text":"With reference to Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), consider the following statements:\nI. All types of UAVs can do vertical landing.\nII. All types of UAVs can do automated hovering.\nIII. All types of UAVs can use battery only as a source of power supply.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Not all UAVs can perform vertical landing or automated hovering. These capabilities depend on the UAV's design, such as whether it is a multirotor, fixed-wing or hybrid UAV.\nUAVs can also use different power sources, so batteries are not the only option.","revisionPoints":"UAV types → Fixed-wing + Multirotor + Hybrid\nVTOL\nHovering\nAutonomous navigation\nUAV applications\nDifferent UAV power sources"},
{"no":43,"text":"In the context of electric vehicle batteries, consider the following elements:\nI. CobaltII. GraphiteIII. LithiumIV. Nickel\nHow many of the above usually make up battery cathodes?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Energy Storage","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"According to the supplied study material, cobalt and nickel are commonly used in cathode materials of lithium-ion batteries. Graphite is commonly used as the anode material, while lithium is treated in the question's context as part of the overall battery chemistry rather than being counted among the listed cathode materials.","revisionPoints":"Li-ion Battery → Cathode: Nickel/Cobalt\nAnode → Graphite\nLithium-ion battery components\nCritical minerals\nBattery chemistry\nIndia's battery-storage ecosystem"},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following:\nI. Cigarette buttsII. Eyeglass lensesIII. Car tyres\nHow many of them contain plastic?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"All three can contain plastic or plastic-based materials:\nCigarette filters commonly contain cellulose acetate.\nEyeglass lenses can be made from plastic polymers.\nCar tyres contain synthetic rubber and other polymeric materials.","revisionPoints":"Plastic is not limited to packaging.\nMicroplastics\nSynthetic polymers\nPlastic pollution\nTyre pollution\nCigarette-filter waste"},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following substances:\nI. EthanolII. NitroglycerineIII. Urea\nCoal gasification technology can be used in the production of how many of them?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry & Energy","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Coal gasification converts coal into syngas, mainly containing carbon monoxide and hydrogen. Syngas can be processed to produce chemicals such as methanol and ammonia, which can subsequently be used in producing products such as ethanol and urea.\nNitroglycerine is not produced through the coal-gasification route in this context.","revisionPoints":"Coal Gasification → Syngas → Chemicals/Fuels/Fertilisers\nSyngas\nCoal-to-chemical technology\nCoal-to-liquid\nHydrogen\nAmmonia and urea production"},
{"no":46,"text":"What is the common characteristic of the chemical substances generally known as CL-20, HMX and LLM-105, which are sometimes talked about in media?","A":"These are alternatives to hydrofluorocarbon refrigerants.","B":"These are explosives in military weapons.","C":"These are high-energy fuels for cruise missiles.","D":"These are fuels for rocket propulsion.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"CL-20, HMX and LLM-105 are high-energy explosive compounds associated with military applications. They belong to the field of energetic materials and can provide high explosive performance.","revisionPoints":"CL-20 + HMX + LLM-105 → High-energy explosives\nEnergetic materials\nDefence technology\nExplosives\nEmerging defence technologies"},
{"no":47,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. It is expected that Majorana 1 chip will enable quantum computing.\nII. Majorana 1 chip has been introduced by Amazon Web Services (AWS).\nIII. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Majorana 1 chip is associated with efforts to develop quantum computing using Majorana-based technology. It was introduced by Microsoft, not Amazon Web Services.\nStatement III is correct because deep learning is a subset of machine learning.","revisionPoints":"AI → Machine Learning → Deep Learning\nQuantum computing\nQubits\nMajorana particles\nQuantum error correction\nClassical vs quantum computing"},
{"no":48,"text":"With reference to monoclonal antibodies, often mentioned in news, consider the following statements:\nI. They are man-made proteins.\nII. They stimulate immunological functions due to their ability to bind to specific antigens.\nIII. They are used in treating viral infections like that of Nipah virus.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Monoclonal antibodies are laboratory-produced antibodies designed to recognize and bind to a specific antigen. Their specificity allows them to be used in various therapeutic applications, including treatment of certain viral infections such as Nipah virus infection.","revisionPoints":"Monoclonal Antibody → Specific antigen → Targeted immune response\nAntibodies vs vaccines\nMonoclonal vs polyclonal antibodies\nTherapeutic antibodies\nAntigen-antibody interaction"},
{"no":49,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. No virus can survive in ocean waters.\nII. No virus can infect bacteria.\nIII. No virus can change the cellular transcriptional activity in host cells.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"All three statements are incorrect.\nViruses are found in ocean waters and marine ecosystems.\nSome viruses, known as bacteriophages, infect bacteria.\nViruses can alter the transcriptional activity of host cells to facilitate their replication.","revisionPoints":"Viruses → Can occur in oceans + Infect bacteria + Alter host-cell activity\nBacteriophages\nViral replication\nHost cells\nMarine viruses"},
{"no":50,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Activated carbon is a good and attractive tool to remove pollutants from effluent streams and to remediate contaminants from various industries.\nStatement II: Activated carbon exhibits a large surface area and a strong potential for adsorbing heavy metals.\nStatement III: Activated carbon can be easily synthesized from environmental wastes with high carbon content.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.","B":"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.","D":"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry & Energy","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Activated carbon has a highly porous structure and large surface area, allowing it to adsorb pollutants, including heavy metals. It can also be produced from carbon-rich wastes, such as agricultural and biomass residues, making it useful for environmental remediation.","revisionPoints":"Activated Carbon → Large Surface Area → Adsorption → Pollution Removal\nAdsorption vs absorption\nWastewater treatment\nHeavy-metal pollution\nBiomass-based activated carbon\nEnvironmental remediation"},
{"no":51,"text":"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. An Ordinance can amend any Central Act.\nII. An Ordinance can abridge a Fundamental Right.\nIII. An Ordinance can come into effect from a back date.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Executive & Legislative Powers","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"An Ordinance issued by the President under Article 123 has the same force and effect as an Act of Parliament while it remains in operation, subject to constitutional limitations. It can amend or repeal a Central law and can have retrospective effect. However, it cannot abridge Fundamental Rights because legislative action cannot violate the Constitution.","revisionPoints":"Ordinance → Article 123 → President\nIssued when Parliament is not in session\nArticle 213 → Governor's Ordinance power\nLimitations on Ordinance power\nDuration and judicial review"},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nState\nDescription\nI. Arunachal Pradesh\nThe capital is named after a fort, and the State has two National Parks\nII. Nagaland\nThe State came into existence on the basis of a Constitutional Amendment Act\nIII. Tripura\nInitially a Part 'C' State, became a centrally administered territory in 1956 and later attained statehood\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"States Reorganization","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Only the description relating to Arunachal Pradesh is correctly matched. Its capital, Itanagar, derives its name from Ita Fort, and the State has two National Parks.\nThe descriptions relating to Nagaland and Tripura do not correctly match their constitutional and administrative history as stated.","revisionPoints":"Arunachal Pradesh → Itanagar → Ita Fort → 2 National Parks\nFormation of North-Eastern States\nState reorganisation\nConstitutional amendments related to Statehood"},
{"no":53,"text":"With reference to India, consider the following:\nI. The Inter-State CouncilII. The National Security CouncilIII. Zonal Councils\nHow many of the above were established as per the provisions of the Constitution of India?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Centre-State Relations","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Inter-State Council is provided for under Article 263, but it is established by a Presidential order rather than being automatically created by the Constitution. The National Security Council and Zonal Councils are also not constitutional bodies.\nTherefore, none of the three was established directly as a constitutional body under the Constitution.","revisionPoints":"Article 263 → Inter-State Council\nInter-State Council is not automatically created by the Constitution\nNational Security Council\nZonal Councils\nConstitutional vs statutory vs executive bodies"},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. The Constitution of India explicitly mentions that in certain spheres the Governor of a State acts in his/her own discretion.\nII. The President of India can, of his/her own, reserve a bill passed by a State Legislature for his/her consideration without it being forwarded by the Governor of the State concerned.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Constitution recognizes certain situations in which the Governor exercises discretion. However, the President cannot independently reserve a State bill for consideration. The bill must be reserved by the Governor under the constitutional procedure.","revisionPoints":"State Bill → Governor reserves → President considers\nGovernor's discretionary powers\nArticles 200–201\nAssent to State bills\nGovernor–President relationship"},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nProvision in the Constitution of India\nStated under\nI. Separation of Judiciary from the Executive in the public services of the State\nDirective Principles of State Policy\nII. Valuing and preserving the rich heritage of our composite culture\nFundamental Duties\nIII. Prohibition of employment of children below the age of 14 years in factories\nFundamental Rights\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The source identifies an inconsistency in the supplied answer.\nPair I is correct: Separation of judiciary from the executive is a Directive Principle under Article 50.\nPair II is correct: Preservation of India's rich cultural heritage is a Fundamental Duty under Article 51A(f).\nPair III is also constitutionally classified as a Fundamental Right under Article 24.\nTherefore, based on the constitutional provisions themselves, all three pairs are correctly matched, even though the supplied answer key gives B. Only two.","revisionPoints":"Article 24 → Prohibition of child labour in factories, etc.\nArticle 50 → Separation of Judiciary from Executive\nArticle 51A(f) → Preserve cultural heritage\nDistinguish:\nFundamental Rights\nDPSPs\nFundamental Duties"},
{"no":56,"text":"With reference to the Constitution of India, if an area in a State is declared as a Scheduled Area under the Fifth Schedule:\nI. The State Government loses its executive power in such areas and a local body assumes total administration.\nII. The Union Government can take over the total administration of such areas under certain circumstances on the recommendations of the Governor.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Declaration of a Scheduled Area does not remove the State Government's executive authority or transfer total administration to a local body. The Fifth Schedule provides a special administrative framework involving the Governor and the President, but it does not give the Union Government the power described in Statement II.","revisionPoints":"Fifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas + Scheduled Tribes\nGovernor's role\nTribal Advisory Councils\nFifth Schedule vs Sixth Schedule\nAdministration of Scheduled Areas"},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to India, consider the following pairs:\nOrganization\nUnion Ministry\nI. The National Automotive Board\nMinistry of Commerce and Industry\nII. The Coir Board\nMinistry of Heavy Industries\nIII. The National Centre for Trade Information\nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\nHow many of the above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The National Centre for Trade Information is associated with the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, while the National Automotive Board is associated with the Ministry of Heavy Industries and the Coir Board comes under the Ministry of MSME.\nThus, only one of the given pairings is correct.","revisionPoints":"Coir Board → Ministry of MSME\nNational Automotive Board → Ministry of Heavy Industries\nNational Centre for Trade Information\nImportant statutory boards and parent ministries"},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following subjects under the Constitution of India:\nI. List I (Union List) in the Seventh Schedule\nII. Extent of the executive power of a State\nIII. Conditions of the Governor's office\nFor a constitutional amendment with respect to which of the above, ratification by the Legislatures of not less than one-half of the States is required?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendment","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Certain constitutional provisions affecting the federal structure require ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures under Article 368. These include matters relating to the distribution of legislative powers and the extent of executive power of States.\nThe conditions of the Governor's office do not require such ratification.","revisionPoints":"Article 368 → Special Amendment → Ratification by at least half of the States\nSeventh Schedule\nDistribution of legislative powers\nExtent of State executive power\nRepresentation of States in Parliament\nSupreme Court and High Courts"},
{"no":59,"text":"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. The Governor of a State is not answerable to any court for the exercise and performance of the powers and duties of his/her office.\nII. No criminal proceedings shall be instituted or continued against the Governor during his/her term of office.\nIII. Members of a State Legislature are not liable to any proceedings in any court in respect of anything said within the House.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Governor","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Under Article 361, the President and Governors enjoy constitutional immunity from being answerable to courts for official acts and from criminal proceedings during their term of office.\nMembers of State Legislatures also enjoy legislative privileges, including protection for speeches and votes in the House.","revisionPoints":"Article 361 → President + Governor → Constitutional immunity\nLegislative privileges\nParliamentary privileges\nFreedom of speech inside the legislature\nJudicial immunity provisions"},
{"no":60,"text":"Consider the following activities:\nI. Production of crude oil\nII. Refining, storage and distribution of petroleum\nIII. Marketing and sale of petroleum products\nIV. Production of natural gas\nHow many of the above activities are regulated by the Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) in India?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Sector Regulation","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) regulates activities related to refining, storage, transportation, distribution, marketing and sale of petroleum and petroleum products within its statutory framework.\nHowever, production of crude oil and production of natural gas are not regulated by PNGRB in the same manner.","revisionPoints":"PNGRB → Downstream petroleum &amp; natural gas sector\nUpstream vs downstream\nPetroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board\nPetroleum pricing\nPetroleum pipelines\nNatural-gas distribution"},
{"no":61,"text":"Suppose the revenue expenditure is ₹80,000 crores and the revenue receipts of the Government are ₹60,000 crores. The Government budget also shows borrowings of ₹10,000 crores and interest payments of ₹6,000 crores. Which of the following statements are correct?\nI. Revenue deficit is ₹20,000 crores.\nII. Fiscal deficit is ₹10,000 crores.\nIII. Primary deficit is ₹4,000 crores.","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure − Revenue Receipts\n= ₹80,000 − ₹60,000= ₹20,000 crore\nThus, Statement I is correct.\nThe given information is insufficient to establish a fiscal deficit of ₹10,000 crore because fiscal deficit depends on total expenditure and total non-debt receipts, not simply the amount of borrowings.\nThe supplied material gives the primary deficit as ₹4,000 crore after deducting interest payments from the fiscal deficit. Therefore, Statement III is treated as correct in the supplied answer.","revisionPoints":"Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure − Revenue Receipts\nPrimary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments\nFiscal deficit represents the government's borrowing requirement.\nRevise revenue deficit, fiscal deficit, primary deficit and effective revenue deficit."},
{"no":62,"text":"India is one of the founding members of the International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC), a multimodal transportation corridor, which will connect","A":"India to Central Asia to Europe via Iran","B":"India to Central Asia via China","C":"India to South-East Asia through Bangladesh and Myanmar","D":"India to Europe through Azerbaijan","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Connectivity Projects","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC) is a multimodal transportation corridor connecting India with Central Asia and Europe through Iran. It uses a combination of sea, rail and road routes to facilitate the movement of goods.","revisionPoints":"INSTC → India → Iran → Central Asia → Europe\nChabahar Port\nINSTC\nBelt and Road Initiative\nIndia's connectivity initiatives\nMultimodal transport corridors"},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Of the two major ethanol producers in the world, Brazil and the USA, Brazil produces more ethanol than the USA.\nStatement II: Unlike the USA where corn is the principal feedstock, sugarcane is the principal feedstock for ethanol production in Brazil.","A":"Both I and II are correct and II explains I.","B":"Both I and II are correct but II does not explain I.","C":"I is not correct but II is correct.","D":"I is correct but II is not correct.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The USA is the world's largest ethanol producer, making Statement I incorrect.\nStatement II is correct: corn is the principal feedstock for ethanol production in the USA, while sugarcane is the principal feedstock in Brazil.","revisionPoints":"USA → Corn-based ethanol\nBrazil → Sugarcane-based ethanol\nBiofuels\nEthanol blending\nE20 target\n1G and 2G ethanol\nEthanol feedstocks"},
{"no":64,"text":"The World Bank warned that India could become one of the first places where wet-bulb temperatures routinely exceed 35°C. Which statements best reflect this implication?\nI. Peninsular India will most likely suffer from flooding, tropical cyclones and droughts.\nII. The survival of animals including humans will be affected as shedding of body heat through perspiration becomes difficult.","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"A wet-bulb temperature of 35°C represents an extremely dangerous level of heat stress. At such conditions, the human body struggles to lose heat through the evaporation of sweat, potentially making prolonged exposure life-threatening.\nStatement I does not specifically follow from the concept of wet-bulb temperature.","revisionPoints":"Wet-bulb temperature → Heat + Humidity → Reduced evaporative cooling\nHeat waves\nHeat stress\nClimate change\nHuman health\nDifference between dry-bulb and wet-bulb temperature"},
{"no":65,"text":"A country's fiscal deficit stands at ₹50,000 crores. It receives ₹10,000 crores through non-debt creating capital receipts. The country's interest liabilities are ₹1,500 crores. What is the gross primary deficit?","A":"₹48,500 crores","B":"₹51,500 crores","C":"₹58,500 crores","D":"None of the above","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"There is a numerical inconsistency in the supplied question/answer.\nUsing the standard definition:\nPrimary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments\nTherefore:\n₹50,000 − ₹1,500 = ₹48,500 crore\nThis gives Option A directly.\nThe ₹10,000 crore of non-debt creating capital receipts should not be subtracted again from the stated fiscal deficit, because fiscal deficit is already defined as the financing gap after accounting for non-debt receipts.","revisionPoints":"Primary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments\nFiscal deficit\nRevenue deficit\nNon-debt creating capital receipts\nGross vs net borrowing\nDo not subtract non-debt capital receipts again from an already stated fiscal deficit."},
{"no":66,"text":"Which of the following statements with regard to recommendations of the 15th Finance Commission are correct?\nI. Recommended ₹4,800 crores (2022–23 to 2025–26) to improve educational outcomes.\nII. 45% of the net proceeds of Union taxes are to be shared with States.\nIII. ₹45,000 crores as performance-based incentive for agricultural reforms.\nIV. Reintroduced tax effort criterion to reward fiscal performance.","A":"I, II and III","B":"I, II and IV","C":"I, III and IV","D":"II, III and IV","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The 15th Finance Commission made recommendations covering education, agricultural reforms and fiscal performance. It also reintroduced the tax effort criterion as one of the factors for incentivising States.\nStatement II is incorrect because the recommended share of the divisible pool for States was 41%, not 45%.","revisionPoints":"15th Finance Commission → 41% States' share\nVertical devolution\nHorizontal devolution\nGrants-in-aid\nTax devolution\nFiscal federalism\nFinance Commission"},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD):\nI. Provides loans and guarantees to middle-income countries.\nII. Works single-handedly to help developing countries reduce poverty.\nIII. Established to help Europe rebuild after World War II.","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The IBRD provides loans, guarantees and related financial support, particularly to middle-income and creditworthy lower-income countries.\nIt was established as part of the Bretton Woods system, initially with a major objective of supporting post-war reconstruction, including Europe.\nStatement II is incorrect because IBRD does not work single-handedly to reduce poverty; it operates as part of the broader World Bank Group.","revisionPoints":"IBRD → World Bank Group → Development Finance\nIBRD\nIDA\nIFC\nMIGA\nICSID\nBretton Woods institutions"},
{"no":68,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding RTGS and NEFT:\nI. RTGS settlement is instantaneous while NEFT takes some time.\nII. RTGS charges customers for inward transactions while NEFT does not.\nIII. RTGS operating hours are restricted on certain days whereas NEFT is not.","A":"I only","B":"I and II","C":"I and III","D":"III only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"RTGS settles transactions individually on a real-time basis, whereas NEFT processes transactions through periodic settlement cycles.\nThe statements regarding charges and restricted operating hours are incorrect in the context of the question.","revisionPoints":"RTGS → Real Time + Gross Settlement\nNEFT → National Electronic Funds Transfer\nIMPS\nUPI\nDigital payment infrastructure\nRTGS vs NEFT"},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nI. United Arab EmiratesII. FranceIII. GermanyIV. SingaporeV. Bangladesh\nHow many countries among the above accept international merchant payments under UPI?","A":"Only two","B":"Only three","C":"Only four","D":"All the five","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"UPI has expanded beyond India through international partnerships and acceptance arrangements. The countries listed have been associated with international UPI-based payment acceptance.","revisionPoints":"UPI → India's digital payment system → International expansion\nUPI International\nCross-border payments\nRuPay\nDigital Public Infrastructure\nIndia's fintech ecosystem"},
{"no":70,"text":"Consider the following statements about PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana:\nI. Targets installation of one crore rooftop solar systems.\nII. MNRE provides training for installation, maintenance and repair.\nIII. Aims to create more than three lakh skilled manpower.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"I and III only","C":"II and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"PM Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana aims to promote rooftop solar installations for one crore households. The programme also includes measures for developing a skilled workforce for the installation, maintenance and repair of rooftop solar systems.","revisionPoints":"PM Surya Ghar → 1 crore households → Rooftop Solar\nMinistry of New and Renewable Energy (MNRE)\nRooftop solar\nSolar photovoltaic technology\nRenewable energy\nIndia's renewable-energy targets"},
{"no":71,"text":"\"Sedition has become my religion\" was the famous statement made by Gandhiji at the time of","A":"Champaran Satyagraha","B":"Publicly violating Salt Law at Dandi","C":"Attending the Second Round Table Conference in London","D":"Launch of the Quit India Movement","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Gandhiji made the statement in the context of his deliberate violation of colonial laws during the Civil Disobedience Movement, particularly the Salt Satyagraha.","revisionPoints":"Salt March → Dandi → 1930 → Civil Disobedience Movement\nSalt Satyagraha\nGandhi-Irwin Pact\nRound Table Conferences\nCivil Disobedience Movement"},
{"no":72,"text":"The famous female figurine known as the 'Dancing Girl', found at Mohenjo-daro, is made of","A":"Carnelian","B":"Clay","C":"Bronze","D":"Gold","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The famous Dancing Girl of Mohenjo-daro is a bronze figurine made using the lost-wax technique. It is an important example of the artistic and metallurgical skills of the Indus Valley Civilization.","revisionPoints":"Dancing Girl → Mohenjo-daro → Bronze → Lost-wax technique\nHarappan art\nSculptures\nSeals\nPottery\nMetallurgy"},
{"no":73,"text":"Who provided legal defence to the people arrested in the aftermath of the Chauri Chaura incident?","A":"C. R. Das","B":"Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant","C":"Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Khwaja Hasan Nizami","D":"M. A. Jinnah","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Following the Chauri Chaura incident, many people were arrested and sentenced. Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant provided legal defence for those accused.","revisionPoints":"Chauri Chaura → 1922 → Violence → Gandhi withdrew Non-Cooperation\nNon-Cooperation Movement\nChauri Chaura incident\nWithdrawal of the movement\nSubsequent political developments"},
{"no":74,"text":"Subsequent to which event did Gandhiji include the upliftment of 'Harijans' in his political and social programme?","A":"The Poona Pact","B":"Gandhi-Irwin Agreement","C":"Arrest of Congress leadership during Quit India Movement","D":"Government of India Act, 1935","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Poona Pact of 1932 was concluded between Gandhi and representatives led by Dr. B. R. Ambedkar regarding political representation of the Depressed Classes. Subsequently, Gandhi placed greater emphasis on the removal of untouchability and social upliftment of oppressed communities.","revisionPoints":"Poona Pact → 1932 → Gandhi + Ambedkar → Representation\nCommunal Award\nSeparate electorates\nPoona Pact\nAnti-untouchability movement"},
{"no":75,"text":"Consider the following fruits:\nI. PapayaII. PineappleIII. Guava\nHow many of the above were introduced into India by the Portuguese during the 16th–17th centuries?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Portuguese played an important role in introducing several New World crops and fruits into India during the Columbian Exchange. Papaya, pineapple and guava were among the plants introduced through these historical trade networks.","revisionPoints":"Portuguese → New World crops → Papaya + Pineapple + Guava\nColumbian Exchange\nPortuguese in India\nIntroduction of crops into India\nNew World crops and their origins"},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nI. United KingdomII. DenmarkIII. New ZealandIV. AustraliaV. Brazil\nHow many of the above countries have more than four time zones?","A":"All the five","B":"Only four","C":"Only three","D":"Only two","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The supplied material states that the answer is three, based on its treatment of countries and their overseas territories.\nHowever, this question requires care because the number of time zones depends on whether overseas territories and dependencies are included. Under the broader conventional treatment, the United Kingdom, Denmark, New Zealand and Australia can each be counted as having more than four time zones when their territories/dependencies are included, while Brazil has four.\nTherefore, the supplied answer should be treated cautiously rather than as an unqualified factual answer.","revisionPoints":"Time zones → Longitudes + International Date Line + territories\nStandard Time\nUTC\nInternational Date Line\nCountries with multiple time zones\nDistinguish mainland time zones from overseas territories."},
{"no":77,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Anadyr (Siberia) and Nome (Alaska) are only a few kilometres apart but are on different calendar days.\nII. When it is Monday in Anadyr, it is Tuesday in Nome.","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Anadyr and Nome are geographically close but lie on opposite sides of the International Date Line. Therefore, they can be on different calendar dates.\nStatement II is incorrect because the specific Monday–Tuesday relationship stated is reversed.","revisionPoints":"International Date Line → Different dates despite geographical proximity\n180° longitude\nInternational Date Line\nTime-zone calculations\nCalendar-day differences"},
{"no":78,"text":"Who among the following was the founder of the 'Self-Respect Movement'?","A":"Periyar E. V. Ramasamy Naicker","B":"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar","C":"Bhaskarrao Jadhav","D":"Dinkarrao Javalkar","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Periyar E. V. Ramasamy founded the Self-Respect Movement in 1925. The movement challenged caste hierarchy, social inequality and discriminatory practices while advocating rationalism and self-respect.","revisionPoints":"Periyar → Self-Respect Movement → 1925 → Social Equality + Rationalism\nDravidian movement\nJustice Party\nSocial reform movements in South India\nAnti-caste movements"},
{"no":79,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nCountry\nResource-rich in\nI. Botswana\nDiamond\nII. Chile\nLithium\nIII. Indonesia\nNickel\nHow many of the above rows are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"All three countries are strongly associated with the listed resources:\nBotswana → Diamond\nChile → Lithium\nIndonesia → Nickel","revisionPoints":"Botswana → Diamond\nChile → Lithium\nIndonesia → Nickel\nCritical minerals\nGlobal mineral distribution\nMineral security\nStrategic importance of critical minerals"},
{"no":80,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nRegion\nCountry\nI. Mallorca\nItaly\nII. Normandy\nSpain\nIII. Sardinia\nFrance\nHow many of the above rows are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"All three pairs are incorrectly matched:\nMallorca → Spain\nNormandy → France\nSardinia → Italy\nTherefore, none of the given pairs is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Mallorca → Spain\nNormandy → France\nSardinia → Italy\nEuropean islands\nEuropean regions\nCountries and important geographical locations"},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Some rare earth elements are used in the manufacture of flat television screens and computer monitors.\nStatement II: Some rare earth elements have phosphorescent properties.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.","C":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.","D":"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Certain rare earth elements are used as phosphors in display technologies. Their ability to emit light after absorbing energy makes them useful in television screens and computer monitors.","revisionPoints":"Rare Earth Elements → Phosphors → Displays\nRare earth elements\nCritical minerals\nApplications of rare earth elements\nIndia's dependence on imports"},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Indian Railways prepared a National Rail Plan (NRP) for a future-ready railway system by 2028.\nII. Kavach is an Automatic Train Protection system developed in collaboration with Germany.\nIII. Kavach consists of RFID tags fitted on tracks in station sections.\nWhich of the above statements are not correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Transport Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The National Rail Plan (NRP) was designed as a long-term plan for developing a future-ready railway system, but the target year stated in Statement I is incorrect.\nKavach is an indigenous Automatic Train Protection (ATP) system developed in India, not in collaboration with Germany. RFID-based equipment forms part of the Kavach system.","revisionPoints":"Kavach → Indigenous Automatic Train Protection System\nNational Rail Plan\nRailway modernisation\nAutomatic train protection\nRailway signalling technology"},
{"no":83,"text":"Consider the following space missions:\nI. Axiom-4II. SpaDeXIII. Gaganyaan\nHow many of the above encourage and support microgravity research?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Axiom-4 and SpaDeX are associated with activities that support microgravity research and experiments. Gaganyaan is primarily India's human spaceflight programme, although scientific experiments are an important part of human spaceflight.","revisionPoints":"Microgravity → Space experiments → Biology + Materials + Technology\nAxiom missions\nSpaDeX\nGaganyaan\nInternational Space Station (ISS)\nIndia's human-spaceflight programme"},
{"no":84,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nAircraft\nDescription\nI. Dornier-228\nMaritime patrol aircraft\nII. IL-76\nSupersonic combat aircraft\nIII. C-17 Globemaster III\nMilitary transport aircraft\nHow many pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All the three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Dornier-228 is used for maritime surveillance/patrol, while the C-17 Globemaster III is a heavy military transport aircraft.\nThe IL-76 is also a military transport aircraft, not a supersonic combat aircraft. Therefore, pairs I and III are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Dornier-228 → Maritime Patrol\nIL-76 → Military Transport\nC-17 → Heavy Military Transport\nIndian defence aircraft\nTransport aircraft\nFighter aircraft\nSurveillance platforms"},
{"no":85,"text":"Artificial way of causing rainfall to reduce air pollution makes use of","A":"Silver iodide and potassium iodide","B":"Silver nitrate and potassium iodide","C":"Silver iodide and potassium nitrate","D":"Silver nitrate and potassium chloride","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Cloud seeding is an artificial technique used to encourage precipitation. Substances such as silver iodide and potassium iodide can act as cloud-seeding agents by providing nuclei around which water droplets or ice crystals can form.","revisionPoints":"Cloud Seeding → Artificial precipitation → Silver Iodide\nCloud formation\nCondensation nuclei\nPrecipitation\nWeather modification"},
{"no":86,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the pardoning power of the President of India:\nI. Exercise of this power can be subjected to limited judicial review.\nII. The President can exercise this power without the advice of the Central Government.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"President","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The President exercises the pardoning power under Article 72 on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers. Although the exercise of this power is subject to limited judicial review, the President cannot exercise it independently of ministerial advice.","revisionPoints":"Article 72 → President → Pardoning Power\nPardon\nReprieve\nRespite\nRemission\nCommutation\nGovernor's power under Article 161"},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. After dissolution of the Lok Sabha, the Speaker continues in office until immediately before the first meeting of the next Lok Sabha.\nII. A Member elected as Speaker must resign from his/her political party.\nIII. The Speaker may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members after at least fourteen days' notice.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Speaker continues in office after the dissolution of the Lok Sabha until immediately before the first meeting of the newly elected Lok Sabha.\nThe Constitution does not require the Speaker to resign from their political party.\nThe Speaker may be removed by a resolution of the Lok Sabha passed by a majority of all the then members of the House, provided that at least 14 days' notice has been given.","revisionPoints":"Speaker → Continues after dissolution\nRemoval → Majority of all then members + 14 days' notice\nSpeaker's powers\nDeputy Speaker\nAnti-defection provisions"},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Questions regarding disqualification under the Tenth Schedule are decided by the President in accordance with the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nII. The Constitution of India does not mention the term \"political party\".\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Anti-Defection Law","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Questions of disqualification under the Tenth Schedule are decided by the Speaker or Chairman of the concerned House, subject to judicial review.\nThe Constitution does not generally establish \"political party\" as a constitutional institution in the manner stated in the first statement. Therefore, Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct.","revisionPoints":"Tenth Schedule → Anti-defection → Speaker/Chairman\nAnti-defection law\nMerger exception\nWhip\nJudicial review"},
{"no":89,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: State Governments have no power to make rules for grant of concessions regarding extraction of minor minerals.\nStatement II: The Central Government has the power to notify minor minerals under the relevant law.\nWhich one is correct?","A":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.","B":"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.","C":"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.","D":"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"State Governments have powers relating to the regulation of minor minerals, including framing rules concerning concessions.\nThe Central Government has the power to declare certain minerals as minor minerals under the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act.","revisionPoints":"Major Minerals → Greater Central role\nMinor Minerals → Significant State Government role\nMMDR Act\nMining leases\nMinor minerals\nCritical minerals"},
{"no":90,"text":"Which organization enacted the Nature Restoration Law (NRL) to tackle climate change and biodiversity loss?","A":"European Union","B":"World Bank","C":"OECD","D":"FAO","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Law","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The European Union adopted the Nature Restoration Law to establish measures for restoring degraded ecosystems and contributing to biodiversity conservation and climate objectives.","revisionPoints":"Nature Restoration Law → European Union → Ecosystem Restoration + Biodiversity\nEU environmental policies\nBiodiversity conservation\nEcosystem restoration\nClimate-change mitigation and adaptation"},
{"no":91,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Panchayats at the intermediate level exist in all States.\nII. Minimum age to become a member of an intermediate Panchayat is 30 years.\nIII. The Chief Minister constitutes the State Finance Commission.\nWhich statements are not correct?","A":"I and II only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Self-Government","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Panchayats at the intermediate level need not exist in every State; the Constitution provides an exception for States with populations not exceeding the prescribed threshold.\nThe minimum age for membership is 21 years, not 30. The Governor, not the Chief Minister, constitutes the State Finance Commission.","revisionPoints":"Panchayati Raj → 73rd Constitutional Amendment\nGram Panchayat\nIntermediate Panchayat\nZila Parishad\nState Election Commission\nState Finance Commission"},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding BIMSTEC:\nI. It consists of seven member States till January 2025.\nII. It came into existence with the Dhaka Declaration (1999).\nIII. Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka, Thailand and Nepal are founding members.\nIV. Tourism sector in BIMSTEC is led by India.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"I and IV","D":"I only","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"BIMSTEC currently consists of seven members: Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Thailand.\nIt was established in 1997, originally as BIST-EC, and not through the 1999 Dhaka Declaration. Nepal and Bhutan joined later.\nIndia leads the Transport and Connectivity sector, while tourism is led by Sri Lanka.","revisionPoints":"BIMSTEC → 7 members → Bay of Bengal region\nFounding members\nBIMSTEC sectors\nIndia's role in BIMSTEC\nBIMSTEC vs SAARC"},
{"no":93,"text":"Who among the following are members of the Jury to select the recipient of the 'Gandhi Peace Prize'?\nI. President of India\nII. Prime Minister of India\nIII. Chief Justice of India\nIV. Leader of Opposition in Lok Sabha","A":"II and IV only","B":"I, II and III","C":"II, III and IV","D":"I and III only","ans":"A","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Awards & Honours","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The jury for the Gandhi Peace Prize includes the Prime Minister as Chairperson and other specified members, including the Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha.\nThe President and Chief Justice of India are not members of the jury in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"Gandhi Peace Prize → Government of India → Peace + Non-violence + Social contribution\nMajor national awards\nPurpose of national awards\nSelection mechanisms"},
{"no":94,"text":"GPS-Aided Geo Augmented Navigation (GAGAN) provides augmentation using ground stations. Which statements are correct?\nI. Provides additional accuracy and integrity.\nII. Enables more uniform and high-quality air traffic management.\nIII. Benefits only aviation and not other transport sectors.","A":"I, II and III","B":"II and III only","C":"I only","D":"I and II only","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"GAGAN is a satellite-based augmentation system that improves the accuracy, integrity and reliability of satellite navigation. It primarily supports aviation but can also have applications in other sectors. Therefore, Statement III is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"GAGAN → GPS augmentation → Accuracy + Integrity → Aviation\nGNSS\nGPS\nNavIC\nGAGAN\nSatellite-based navigation systems"},
{"no":95,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the AI Action Summit held in Paris (February 2025):\nI. Co-chaired with India and built on the Bletchley Park (2023) and Seoul (2024) Summits.\nII. USA and UK signed the declaration on inclusive and sustainable AI.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Artificial Intelligence","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The AI Action Summit in Paris was co-chaired by France and India and continued the international dialogue established through the Bletchley AI Safety Summit and Seoul AI Summit.\nThe USA and UK did not sign the declaration in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"AI Summits → Bletchley 2023 → Seoul 2024 → Paris 2025\nAI governance\nAI safety\nResponsible AI\nInternational cooperation on artificial intelligence"},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nInternational Year\nYear\nI. International Year of the Woman Farmer\n2026\nII. International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism\n2027\nIII. International Year of Peace and Trust\n2025\nIV. International Year of Asteroid Awareness and Planetary Defence\n2029\nHow many pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All the four","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"International Observances","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Among the listed pairs, International Year of Peace and Trust — 2025 and International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism — 2027 are correctly matched according to the given framework.\nThe other two pairings are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"2025 → International Year of Peace and Trust\nUN-designated International Years\nAgriculture-related international observances\nTourism-related international observances\nPeace and space-related international observances"},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding BRICS:\nI. The 16th BRICS Summit was held under Russia's chairmanship at Kazan.\nII. Indonesia has become a full member of BRICS.\nIII. Theme: \"Strengthening Multiculturalism for Just Global Development and Security.\"\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I and II","B":"II and III","C":"I and III","D":"I only","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The 16th BRICS Summit was held in Kazan, Russia, in 2024, under Russia's chairmanship.\nThe statements regarding Indonesia's full membership and the quoted theme are incorrect in the context of this question.","revisionPoints":"16th BRICS Summit → Kazan → Russia → 2024\nBRICS expansion\nBRICS New Development Bank\nIndia's role in BRICS\nBRICS member countries"},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements about Lokpal:\nI. Lokpal applies only to public servants in India, not those posted abroad.\nII. Only the Chief Justice of India can be Lokpal Chairperson.\nIII. Chairperson or Member must be at least 45 years old.\nIV. Lokpal cannot inquire into allegations against a sitting Prime Minister.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"III only","B":"II and III","C":"I and IV","D":"None of the above","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Anti-Corruption Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The Lokpal framework can cover public servants even when they are serving outside India, subject to the law.\nThe Chairperson need not be the Chief Justice of India; the law allows specified categories of persons to be appointed.\nA Chairperson or Member must have attained the age of 45 years.\nLokpal can inquire into allegations against a sitting Prime Minister, subject to specified safeguards and exclusions.","revisionPoints":"Lokpal → Anti-corruption institution → Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013\nJurisdiction of Lokpal\nPrime Minister's coverage\nAppointment of Lokpal\nLokayukta"},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the first Kho Kho World Cup:\nI. Held in Delhi, India.\nII. Indian men defeated Nepal 78–40 in the final.\nIII. Indian women defeated Nepal 54–36 in the final.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II and III only","C":"I and III only","D":"I, II and III","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"The inaugural Kho Kho World Cup was held in New Delhi, India, in 2025. India won both the men's and women's titles, defeating Nepal in their respective finals with the scores stated.","revisionPoints":"First Kho Kho World Cup → New Delhi → 2025 → India won both titles\nTraditional Indian sports\nInternational sporting events\nIndia's recent sporting achievements"},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. In the final of the 45th Chess Olympiad (2024), Gukesh Dommaraju became the world's youngest winner after defeating Ian Nepomniachtchi.\nII. Abhimanyu Mishra (USA) holds the record as the world's youngest Grandmaster.\nWhich statements are correct?","A":"I only","B":"II only","C":"Both I and II","D":"Neither I nor II","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2025"],"expl":"Abhimanyu Mishra holds the record for becoming the youngest chess Grandmaster.\nStatement I is incorrect because the Chess Olympiad is a team event. Gukesh's world-title achievement came later in the World Chess Championship, not by defeating Ian Nepomniachtchi in a Chess Olympiad final.","revisionPoints":"Abhimanyu Mishra → Youngest Grandmaster\nChess Olympiad 2024\nGukesh Dommaraju\nWorld Chess Championship\nImportant chess records"},
];
